Galaxy S24 postmarketOS (Claude, 2026-11-15): without timer scheduling, write whole periods only,
one snd_pcm_writei() each. The phone's ABOX DSP (Samsung "calliope" virtual PCMs) takes every write
as one whole period (the driver sends one "VPCM ACK" per write and the firmware books 3840 bytes for
each), so the partial writes PulseAudio made whenever it had resampled or a client's chunks didn't
line up with periods were counted twice: the DSP's mixer overflowed ("overflow detected. drain
sink"), drained and played silence, which was audible as stutter. Steady, period-sized writes (as
aplay and Android do) never trigger it. Mmap and timer-scheduled sinks are untouched.

--- a/src/modules/alsa/alsa-sink.c
+++ b/src/modules/alsa/alsa-sink.c
@@ -887,6 +887,14 @@
         n_bytes -= u->hwbuf_unused;
         polled = false;
 
+        /* Whole periods only when scheduling by period interrupts (see the patch header). */
+        if (!u->use_tsched) {
+            n_bytes -= n_bytes % u->fragment_size;
+
+            if (n_bytes <= 0)
+                break;
+        }
+
         for (;;) {
             snd_pcm_sframes_t frames;
             void *p;
@@ -894,8 +902,12 @@
 
 /*         pa_log_debug("%lu frames to write", (unsigned long) frames); */
 
-            if (u->memchunk.length <= 0)
-                pa_sink_render(u->sink, n_bytes, &u->memchunk);
+            if (u->memchunk.length <= 0) {
+                if (u->use_tsched)
+                    pa_sink_render(u->sink, n_bytes, &u->memchunk);
+                else
+                    pa_sink_render_full(u->sink, u->fragment_size, &u->memchunk);
+            }
 
             pa_assert(u->memchunk.length > 0);
 
